    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>The Texas eviction process has four stages: a written notice to vacate (three days unless the lease says otherwise), a lawsuit in the justice court where the property sits, a trial and judgment, and, if the tenant still does not leave, a writ of possession carried out by a constable or sheriff. A landlord can never skip the court steps by changing locks to force a move-out, removing belongings or cutting off utilities.</p>
<p>The rules changed for suits filed on or after January 1, 2026, when Senate Bill 38 and new Texas Rules of Civil Procedure 510 took effect. This guide covers the current process for landlords, what tenants should know, a timeline and Harris County costs. It is general information, not legal advice; for a specific case, talk to a Texas landlord-tenant attorney.</p>

<h2>What changed in 2026: SB 38 in brief</h2>
<p>The 2025 Legislature passed SB 38, a broad rewrite of Chapter 24 of the Property Code. Most of it applies to eviction suits filed on or after January 1, 2026; cases filed earlier stay under the old rules. The Texas Supreme Court adopted amended eviction rules (TRCP 510) on the same schedule. The changes that matter most to small landlords:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>New notice delivery rules.</strong> Posting the notice on the outside of the door is gone. Notice can be mailed, hand-delivered to a resident 16 or older, left inside the unit in a conspicuous place, or sent by email or other electronic means if the lease or another written agreement allows it.</li>
<li><strong>Notice to pay rent or vacate.</strong> If the only default is unpaid rent and the tenant was not late before that month, the notice must give the tenant the option to pay or move out.</li>
<li><strong>Clear day counting.</strong> The day the notice is given does not count, weekends and holidays do, and a period that ends on a weekend or holiday rolls to the next business day.</li>
<li><strong>Faster service.</strong> The constable or sheriff must make a diligent effort to serve the petition within five business days and the writ within five business days of issuance. If they miss it, the landlord may use another properly trained law enforcement officer.</li>
<li><strong>No local add-ons.</strong> Justice courts cannot require mediation or pretrial conferences, or demand petition content beyond what the statewide rules require.</li>
<li><strong>Rent during appeals.</strong> Any tenant who appeals must pay rent into the court registry, however the appeal was filed.</li>
<li><strong>Squatters.</strong> A summary disposition process lets courts decide cases against people who entered without permission without a full trial, if the facts are not genuinely disputed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Step 1: Give the right written notice</h2>
<p>Property Code section 24.005 requires at least three days' written notice before you file, unless a written lease sets a shorter or longer period. Many Texas leases shorten it, so read yours. Pick the right form:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Notice to pay rent or vacate:</strong> required when the only default is nonpayment and the tenant was current before the month the notice is given.</li>
<li><strong>Notice to vacate:</strong> used for other lease violations, for tenants with a history of late payment, and for holdovers.</li>
</ul>
<p>If the tenant is holding over on a month-to-month lease, you must also properly terminate the tenancy under section 91.001, which generally requires at least one month's notice unless the parties agreed otherwise. Federal rules can add time too. Some federally backed or subsidized properties require a 30-day notice; under SB 38, you can still file once the state notice period runs, but the writ cannot be served until the federal period has passed.</p>
<p>Keep proof of delivery: a mailing receipt, a dated photo of the notice inside the unit, or the email you sent. At trial, someone with personal knowledge must testify how the notice was delivered.</p>

<h2>Step 2: File the eviction suit in justice court</h2>
<p>Evictions are filed in the justice court for the precinct where the property is located. Harris County has 16 justice courts across eight precincts, two courts per precinct, and you can find the right one and its filing options through the <a href="https://www.jp.hctx.net/">Harris County Justice Courts</a> website. Fort Bend, Montgomery and other counties run their own JP courts.</p>
<p>You file a sworn petition that includes the content required by TRCP 510: the property address, the grounds for eviction, how and when notice was given, and any unpaid rent you are seeking. You can join a claim for unpaid rent, but other damages, such as repair costs, belong in a separate suit. The court issues a citation, and the constable serves the tenant.</p>

<h2>Step 3: The trial</h2>
<p>The trial must be held no earlier than the 10th day and no later than the 21st day after the petition is filed, and not sooner than four days after the tenant is served. The court cannot postpone it more than seven days unless both sides agree in writing. Tenants are not required to file a written answer, but a tenant who does not show up risks a default judgment. Either side can request a jury.</p>
<p>Bring the lease, the notice and proof of delivery, a rent ledger, photos and any written communications. Judges focus on a few questions: was there a valid lease or tenancy, did the tenant default or hold over, and was proper notice given? A defective notice is the most common reason landlords lose and have to start over.</p>
<p>In justice court, a landlord can appear personally or through an authorized agent, such as a property manager, who does not need to be a lawyer. If both sides agree, the judge may allow remote appearances.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Judgment and the five-day appeal window</h2>
<p>If you win, the court signs a judgment for possession and any unpaid rent and court costs it awards. Either party then has five days after the judgment is signed to appeal to county court by filing a bond, a cash deposit or a statement of inability to afford court costs. Under SB 38, a tenant who appeals must swear the appeal has merit and is not filed for delay.</p>
<p>During an appeal the tenant must pay rent into the court registry, starting within five days of filing the appeal and then each rental period. If a tenant does not pay, the landlord can request a writ of possession, which the court must issue without a hearing. The county court must hold the appeal trial within 21 days after it receives the case file.</p>

<h2>Step 5: Writ of possession</h2>
<p>If no appeal is perfected, you can request a writ of possession. Under TRCP 510.8 it cannot issue before the sixth day after the judgment is signed. The constable then posts a written warning on the front door at least 24 hours before executing the writ, as Property Code section 24.0061 requires. On execution day, the officer supervises removal of the tenant and their belongings and can authorize the landlord to change the locks.</p>
<p>Request the writ promptly. Under the court rules, a writ generally cannot issue more than 60 days after judgment without good cause, and even when you are fairly sure the tenant has left, having the writ executed protects you from claims that you wrongfully removed property.</p>

<h2>Texas eviction timeline at a glance</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Typical timing</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Notice to vacate or pay or vacate</td><td>3 days (or the lease period)</td><td>File after the period ends; weekend endings roll to the next business day</td></tr>
<tr><td>Service of the petition</td><td>Within 5 business days of filing</td><td>Diligent-effort deadline for the constable or sheriff</td></tr>
<tr><td>Trial</td><td>Day 10 to 21 after filing</td><td>At least 4 days after the tenant is served</td></tr>
<tr><td>Appeal window</td><td>5 days after judgment is signed</td><td>Tenant must pay rent into the registry to stay</td></tr>
<tr><td>Writ of possession issues</td><td>Day 6 or later after judgment</td><td>Officer must serve within 5 business days of issuance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Writ executed</td><td>At least 24 hours after posted warning</td><td>Landlord may change locks after execution</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>An uncontested case often wraps up in about a month. Contested cases, appeals and service problems add time.</p>

<h2>What an eviction costs in Harris County</h2>
<p>According to the Harris County Justice Courts' posted fee schedule (effective January 2025; check for updates before filing):</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Filing the petition:</strong> $54 in court costs plus an $85 constable service fee, $139 total, with service fees charged for each defendant served.</li>
<li><strong>Writ of possession:</strong> $5 in court costs plus a $155 service fee, $160 total.</li>
<li><strong>Jury fee:</strong> $22 if a jury is requested.</li>
</ul>
<p>The bigger costs are usually lost rent, turnover repairs and attorney fees if you hire counsel or face an appeal. Run those in our <a href="/tools/rental-property-calculator/">rental property calculator</a> as part of your vacancy and credit-loss assumptions, as covered in <a href="/blog/how-to-analyze-a-rental-property/">how to analyze a rental property</a>.</p>

<h2>Self-help evictions are illegal</h2>
<p>Texas law is strict about landlords who try to force tenants out without a court order. Under Property Code chapter 92:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Utility cutoffs (section 92.008):</strong> a landlord cannot interrupt utilities the tenant pays to the provider, or utilities the landlord supplies, except for bona fide repairs, construction or an emergency. Submetered electricity has its own narrow rules.</li>
<li><strong>Lockouts (section 92.0081):</strong> a landlord may change locks for delinquent rent only if the lease allows it and written notice is given in advance (at least five days by mail, or three days by hand or posted inside the main entry door). The tenant must get a new key at any hour, whether or not they pay. Locks can be changed only once per rental period, and never while the tenant is inside.</li>
<li><strong>Removing doors, windows, appliances or belongings</strong> to drive a tenant out is prohibited, except for genuine repairs or abandoned property.</li>
</ul>
<p>Violations let the tenant recover possession or end the lease, plus actual damages, one month's rent plus $1,000, court costs and attorney's fees. A tenant can also go to justice court for a writ of re-entry or a writ restoring utilities.</p>
<p>A smart lock that lets you issue and revoke codes remotely makes it easy to follow the key rules and to rekey cleanly after a writ is executed.</p>
