<h2>What tenants should know</h2>
<p>A notice to vacate is not an eviction. Only a judge can order you out, and only a constable or sheriff can remove you. Read the citation, go to the hearing, and bring rent receipts, photos and messages. If you appeal, you must pay rent into the court registry on time or the landlord can get a writ quickly. Texas Law Help and local legal aid groups can help you prepare.</p>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>The Texas eviction process rewards landlords who get the paperwork right. Use the correct notice, deliver it a way the 2026 rules allow, file in the right justice court, show up with documents and let the constable handle possession. Screening tenants well and budgeting for vacancy, covered in our <a href="/category/investing/">investing guides</a>, is still cheaper than any eviction.</p>
